 "source_url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DfSxp-ez93g/",
 "title": "Stop Copied Content in Business",
 "topic": "content creation",
 "summary": "The creator criticizes business content creators on social media who copy templates, scripts, and hooks from others instead of using original ideas. He claims this makes them look untrustworthy to serious buyers, and advises prioritizing original thinking over chasing views.",
 "key_points": [
  "The creator claims that many people in business outsource their thinking to AI or template libraries, regurgitating other people's stuff to get views.",
  "The creator shows examples of videos with identical scripts and hooks appearing on feeds within minutes of each other.",
  "Social media platforms are geared to show users more of what they watch or engage with, meaning audiences will spot duplicate content right away.",
  "The creator advises prioritizing original ideas instead of copying viral content for views.",
  "The creator recommends listening to @calebralston for advice on this topic.",
  "The creator states that their own business pulls well into seven figures a year with no viral videos and few followers, proving that original ideas matter more than follower counts or millions of views."
 ],
 "content": "Let me show you what fucking pisses me off about people in business on social media nowadays. Take a look at these examples. And now watch these two videos. See this is what happens when people just outsource their thinking to I don't know, AI or some fucking template library that they've purchased off someone who's just gotten really good at regurgitating other people's stuff so that you buy the template library. It drives me nuts because you should be prioritizing your original ideas. I don't care if there's something that you can regurgitate that will get you the views. Fuck the views. The only way to truly cut through is to have your original ideas. See, I love this guy Caleb. He's constantly telling us to stop regurgitating other people's shit, focus on your own unique views and opinions, and if it's contrarian, even better. And the main reason that'll work is because you're no longer just spitting other people's bullshit. The worst part about the examples that I showed you earlier is that they all appeared on the feed at the same time. So when you're regurgitating other people's shit for views and comments and likes and whatever the hell it is, have a think about this. Instagram, TikTok, they're all geared to show you more of the content that you just watched or engaged with. So how trustworthy does it make you when you're the first video there, and then I scroll down and I see the second one, I scroll down and I see the third one, and they're all fucking identical. Instagram reel/post by Johnny Englezos. Text / caption: Okay, I’m sure I’ll get called an asshole for this one. But seriously… if your content strategy involves regurgitating other people’s stuff because you saw it get some engagement… Take your shoe off and give yourself a good whack across the head. You do realise that these platforms are geared to show you more of what you watch yeah? So when you put out something similar to a video that performed well… the same people see all of the dupes, too. This morning I saw the four examples I show you here all within minutes of each other. God knows how many more copycats I would’ve found if I kept scrolling. When it comes to creating content, the main guy you should listen to is @calebralston - that mfer has this stuff down to an art. But the main takeaway is… that you should stop outsourcing your thinking to AI or a “viral hooks” library and start being original. You don’t need the followers to make money. You don’t need millions of views either. My biz pulls well into 7 figures a year and I’ve never gone viral once (and I have fuck all followers too). But I guess it’s because my ideas are my own 🤷‍♂️ Make a note of this post.",
